Write implementation.kt directly from this prompt without exploring outside the workspace and without long deliberation: nothing can be compiled or run while generating, no other project's files are reference material, and the design below is already decided.
Generated Kotlin ABI (package real.posting.client, all public data classes with positional primary constructors): Header(name: kotlin.String, value: kotlin.String); Request(method: HttpMethod, url: kotlin.String, headers: cott_runtime.CottList<Header>, body: kotlin.String, timeout_ms: kotlin.UInt); Response(status: kotlin.UShort, url: kotlin.String, headers: cott_runtime.CottList<Header>, body: kotlin.String); sealed interface HttpMethod with objects Get, Head, Post, Put, Patch, Delete, Options and data class Custom(name: kotlin.String); sealed interface PostingError with data classes InvalidArguments(message), InvalidRequest(message) and NetworkFailed(message), each taking one kotlin.String; real.posting.client.MAX_RESPONSE_BODY_BYTES is a kotlin.ULong. cott_runtime.CottList(iterable) copies any Iterable and is itself a kotlin.collections.List; results are cott_runtime.Ok(value) and cott_runtime.Err(error). The facade treats any thrown exception as a contract violation, so catch every java.io.IOException and RuntimeException inside the implementation and return NetworkFailed.
Decided design of send_request: (1) Validate first and return InvalidRequest before any I/O: timeout_ms == 0u; URL prefix; an explicit character scan of the whole URL that rejects every char that is not an RFC 3986 character (unreserved, reserved gen-delims/sub-delims, and '%' only when followed by two hex digits) -- java.net.URI accepts raw non-ASCII, so never rely on URI for this; a non-empty host (parse authority by hand: skip userinfo up to the last '@', host is up to ':' or the closing ']' of an IP literal, port: empty means the scheme default, otherwise digits only with leading zeros allowed, the decimal value is used, a non-digit port or a value above 65535 is InvalidRequest; port 0 is not rejected, it is attempted and fails as NetworkFailed; parse the digits with a BigInteger or a length-safe loop so a very long run of zeros or digits cannot overflow); header names non-empty HTTP tokens; header values without CR or LF and without any character above U+007F (InvalidRequest, no connection); Custom method name an HTTP token.
(2) Transport: java.net.Socket and javax.net.ssl.SSLSocketFactory (https) with a hand-written HTTP/1.1 writer and parser; java.net.HttpURLConnection cannot send Custom or PATCH methods and java.net.http.HttpClient rejects Host and Content-Length headers, so neither is used. Connect with Socket.connect(InetSocketAddress, timeout_ms) and set Socket.soTimeout to timeout_ms. Request target is the URL path plus '?' and the query exactly as written (keep an empty '?'; '/' when the path is empty; drop the fragment; never re-encode or decode percent-escapes). Send Host as the URL host exactly as written (case kept, IPv6 literal with its brackets, userinfo ignored) with ':port' only when the port is not the scheme default, unless the headers being sent contain a Host header (ASCII case-insensitive); on a request after a cross-origin redirect the Host header was stripped, so the URL's own host is sent; then the headers in order, Content-Length only when the body is non-empty, Connection: close, then the UTF-8 body.
(3) Response: parse the status line strictly (anything not starting with HTTP/1. and a 3-digit status is NetworkFailed), header lines in received order with duplicates kept (a header line whose field name is not one or more HTTP token characters immediately followed by ':' is NetworkFailed: an empty name, a name with a non-ASCII byte, '@' or any other non-token character, whitespace before the colon, or no colon at all; split at the first colon, remove only leading and trailing SP and HTAB from the value, never other whitespace such as U+00A0 or FF; header names are kept as received), then the body: none whenever the request wire method is exactly the string HEAD (HttpMethod.Head or Custom("HEAD")) whatever Content-Length or Transfer-Encoding say, and none for statuses 204 and 304, chunked when Transfer-Encoding says chunked, else Content-Length, else read to close. Count body bytes against MAX_RESPONSE_BODY_BYTES while reading and return NetworkFailed when exceeded. A connection closed inside the headers, inside a body of known length, or before the last chunk is NetworkFailed. A final status outside 100-599 is NetworkFailed.
(4) UTF-8 decoding: new String(bytes, UTF_8) and CharsetDecoder emit a single U+FFFD for an encoded surrogate ED A0..BF xx, where Unicode maximal-subpart replacement needs three (ED A0 80 -> three U+FFFD), so write a small hand-written decoder over the ByteArray. Well-formed sequences are exactly 00..7F; C2..DF 80..BF; E0 A0..BF 80..BF; E1..EC 80..BF 80..BF; ED 80..9F 80..BF; EE..EF 80..BF 80..BF; F0 90..BF 80..BF 80..BF; F1..F3 80..BF 80..BF 80..BF; F4 80..8F 80..BF 80..BF. At each position read a lead byte; when it is a valid lead, consume continuation bytes one at a time while each is in the range allowed at that position; if the sequence is complete emit the code point, otherwise emit ONE U+FFFD for the consumed prefix (the valid lead plus accepted continuations) and resume at the offending byte without consuming it; a byte that is not a valid lead (80..C1, F5..FF) is one U+FFFD by itself; ED followed by A0..BF stops after ED, so each of the three bytes is its own U+FFFD. Keep a BOM (EF BB BF -> U+FEFF) and NUL characters unchanged.
(5) Redirects: at most 10 for Get and Head on statuses 301, 302, 303, 307, 308 that carry a Location header. Never use java.net.URI.resolve for Location: it implements RFC 2396 (base http://h/dir/redir?x=1 with "?q=1" gives http://h/dir/?q=1, and http://h/redir with "../../echo" gives http://h/../../echo). Hand-write RFC 3986 section 5.2: split the reference into scheme/authority/path/query/fragment, merge paths (base path up to and including its last '/'; empty reference path keeps the base path and, when the reference has no query, the base query), run remove_dot_segments (section 5.2.4; empty segments are kept, '..' above the root is dropped), keep the reference fragment. A Location containing any character outside the URL character set of step (1) (including '%' not followed by two hex digits), or whose resolved scheme is not http/https, is not followed and the 3xx response is returned. Do not follow when the target leaves https for http. Compare origins with the scheme and host lower-cased and default ports (80/443) filled in; when the origin changes drop Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization and Host (ASCII case-insensitive) from the headers sent afterwards. Response.url is request.url unchanged when no redirect was followed (never normalized); after a followed redirect it is the resolved Location including its fragment. The base fragment is never inherited. Examples that must hold: base http://h/dir/redir with "?q=1" -> http://h/dir/redir?q=1; base http://h/redir with "../../echo" -> http://h/echo; base http://h/a/b/c with "..//g" -> http://h/a//g.
(6) Round-4 wire and URL decisions; these override anything above that conflicts. (a) Status line: 'HTTP/1.' digit, SP, exactly three digits, then either end of line or SP followed by any (possibly empty) reason phrase; 'HTTP/1.1 200' without a reason is valid. (b) Interim responses: a response with status 100 or 102..199 is read together with its header block and discarded, then the next response is read from the same connection; status 101 is NetworkFailed; the first other response is the final one; a final status outside 100-599 is NetworkFailed. (c) Chunked bodies: the chunk-size line is validated byte by byte: it starts with one or more hexadecimal digits (no leading whitespace, no '0x', no sign); after the digits it may end, or continue with SP/HTAB characters, or with optional SP/HTAB and then ';' followed by an ignored extension of any bytes; any other character after the digits is NetworkFailed, so '2', '2 ', '2\t', '2;a=b' and '2 ; a=b' are accepted and ' 2', '0x2', '2x' and '' are NetworkFailed; the size value is parsed from the digits with overflow protection; after the zero-size chunk read and discard trailer fields until the empty line, none of them go into Response.headers; a connection closed after the zero chunk and before that final empty line is NetworkFailed; a chunk not followed by CRLF is NetworkFailed. (d) Multiple Location header fields: the Location value is ALL Location fields (ASCII case-insensitive name) in received order joined with ', ' (comma, space); that value contains a space so it fails the URL character check and the 3xx is returned unfollowed. (e) Request target path: remove_dot_segments (RFC 3986 section 5.2.4, applied to the literal path only, percent-encoded dots are NOT decoded) is applied to the initial URL path and to every followed Location path before it is written as the request target, so http://h/./echo is requested as /echo and http://h/a/./b/../echo as /a/echo; Response.url stays request.url exactly as given when no redirect was followed, and after a followed redirect is the resolved Location. (f) Location scheme is case-insensitive: HTTP://x and Https://x are accepted, and the resolved URL (the next request, Response.url, the origin comparison and the https-to-http check) uses the lower-case scheme; the rest of the resolved URL keeps its case. A Location whose port is not digits or is above 65535 is not followed (3xx returned); an empty port, or leading zeros, are handled by the same port rule as the request URL. (g) Host header and origin comparison use the decimal port without leading zeros, and the port is omitted when it equals the scheme default (http://h:080/ sends 'Host: h'; http://h:0039525/ sends 'Host: h:39525'). (h) Received header text decoding, decided over the WHOLE header block of the final response (all names and values, after the interim responses are discarded): keep the raw header bytes of every line; names are always ASCII HTTP tokens by the rule in step (3), so only values can contain bytes >= 0x80; if every byte in the block is below 0x80 decode as ASCII; otherwise if every value is well-formed UTF-8 (strict Table 3-7 ranges from step 4, no replacement) decode ALL of them as UTF-8; otherwise decode ALL values as ISO-8859-1 (names are ASCII already). Do not decode line by line and do not mix encodings: a block with X-Latin1: caf\xe9 and X-Utf8: caf\xc3\xa9 decodes both as Latin-1 (café and cafÃ©). So _readLine must return raw bytes (ByteArray), and the status line and chunk-size lines are ASCII-parsed from them. (i) SP and HTAB trimming of a header value is done on bytes or on the decoded text, but never before the decoding decision changes which bytes are present.
